## Data Route Handler Pattern (Authenticated)

All non-auth BFF routes follow this pattern — validate session, extract ID token, forward to AWS:

> **Important:** The AWS API Gateway JWT authorizer validates **ID tokens** (not access tokens).
> Cognito access tokens use the User Pool URL as the audience, but the authorizer checks
> audience against the **client ID**, which only matches in ID tokens. The BFF must send
> the ID token as the Bearer token to AWS.

```typescript
// src/app/api/players/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export async function GET(request: NextRequest): Promise<NextResponse> {
  // 1. Validate session and extract ID token
  const session = await getSession(request);
  if (!session?.idToken) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Unauthorized' }, { status: 401 });
  }

  // 2. Check if ID token is expired → refresh if needed
  if (isTokenExpired(session.idToken)) {
    const newTokens = await refreshTokens(session.refreshToken);
    if (!newTokens) {
      return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Session expired' }, { status: 401 });
    }
    session.idToken = newTokens.id_token;
    // TODO: Update session cookie with new tokens
  }

  // 3. Forward to AWS API with ID token as Bearer
  const response = await fetch(`${process.env.AWS_API_BASE_URL}/players`, {
    headers: {
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${session.idToken}`,
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    },
  });

  // 4. Handle errors gracefully
  if (!response.ok) {
    const status = response.status === 403 ? 403 : response.status >= 500 ? 502 : response.status;
    return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Request failed' }, { status });
  }

  // 5. Return response
  const data = await response.json();
  return NextResponse.json(data);
}
```

## Token Refresh (Server-Side)

When the access token expires, the BFF refreshes it transparently:

```typescript
async function refreshTokens(refreshToken: string): Promise<TokenSet | null> {
  const response = await fetch(`${process.env.COGNITO_DOMAIN}/oauth2/token`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: new URLSearchParams({
      grant_type: 'refresh_token',
      client_id: process.env.COGNITO_CLIENT_ID!,
      refresh_token: refreshToken,
    }),
  });

  if (!response.ok) return null;
  return response.json();
}
```

## Conventions

- Use Next.js Route Handler conventions: `route.ts` files exporting `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`.
- Type all request/response payloads using shared types from `src/types/`.
- Handle AWS API errors gracefully — map them to appropriate HTTP status codes for the client.
- Log errors server-side but return sanitized messages to the client (no AWS internals leak).
- **Never return raw tokens to the browser.** The session cookie is the only auth artifact the browser sees.

## Environment Variables

BFF routes rely on server-only env vars (NOT prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`):

```
# AWS API
AWS_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# Cognito
COGNITO_DOMAIN=https://your-pool.auth.us-east-1.amazoncognito.com
COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=your-app-client-id
COGNITO_REDIRECT_URI=https://your-vercel-domain.com/api/auth/callback
COGNITO_LOGOUT_URI=https://your-vercel-domain.com

# Session encryption
SESSION_SECRET=a-32-char-minimum-secret-for-iron-session
```

These are configured in Vercel's environment settings per deployment environment (dev, preview, production). **Never commit these values to the repository.**
